---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
import InstallPicker from '../components/InstallPicker.astro';

// The install path is Neuman's, and it is Nix: one flake, a NixOS module for
// the server and a home-manager module for the client. Every command here is
// from docs/setup.md, checked against nix/module/{default,client}.nix — change
// them there first, then here.
//
// Each section is one picker and the panels under it: a square per platform,
// and the install of the one picked. A new platform is a tile in the list, a
// `-panel` element below it, and nothing else.

const code = 'mt-2 bg-nord-950 border border-nord-800 rounded-lg p-4 overflow-x-auto text-[12.5px] leading-relaxed font-mono text-nord-300';
const ic = 'font-mono text-[12.5px] text-frost-300';
const stepLabel = 'text-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-nord-500';
const noteList = 'space-y-2 text-sm font-light text-nord-400 leading-relaxed';
const noteDot = 'text-frost-500 shrink-0';

const flakeInput = `neuman.url = "github:lukebodmer/neuman";
neuman.inputs.nixpkgs.follows = "nixpkgs";`;

const serverConfig = `imports = [ inputs.neuman.nixosModules.neuman ];

services.neuman = {
  enable = true;
  adminSocketAccess = [ "you" ];   # who may create the first administrator
};`;

const clientConfig = `imports = [ inputs.neuman.homeModules.neuman-client ];

programs.neuman-client = {
  enable = true;
  server = "https://neuman.example.com";   # your appliance
};`;

// The Ubuntu packages are versioned with Neuman; one place so the panels cannot
// link a file whose name they do not know.
const debVersion = '0.1.0';
const debFile = `neuman_${debVersion}_amd64.deb`;
const debUrl = `/neuman/${debFile}`;
const clientDebFile = `neuman-client_${debVersion}_amd64.deb`;
const clientDebUrl = `/neuman/${clientDebFile}`;
---

<Base
  title="Download — Private AI Solutions"
  description="Install Neuman from its Nix flake: the server as a NixOS module, the client through home-manager."
>
  <main class="max-w-4xl w-full mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 py-16">

    <!-- Without JavaScript the tabs do nothing, so every panel stays visible
         and the page reads as three stacked installs. With it, the markup's
         `hidden` is the default and nothing opens until a tile is clicked. -->
    <noscript>
      <style is:inline>{`[id$="-panel"] { display: block !important; }`}</style>
    </noscript>

    <PageHeader eyebrow="Neuman" title="Download" accent="Neuman" class="mb-10">
      <p>
        Neuman is two programs and one flake. The <span class="text-nord-100">server</span>
        runs the models and keeps the record, on the appliance you own; the
        <span class="text-nord-100">client</span> runs on each machine a person works
        at. Pick a platform to see its install.
      </p>
    </PageHeader>

    <div class="space-y-6">

      <!-- The server -->
      <section class="p-6 sm:p-8 bg-nord-900 border border-nord-800 rounded-xl">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-nord-100 flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="text-frost-400 text-sm">◆</span>
          The server
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-nord-400">
          Runs the models and keeps the record. One per appliance.
        </p>

        <div class="mt-7">
          <InstallPicker
            label="Server platform"
            installs={[
              { id: 'server-nixos', name: 'NixOS', detail: 'x86_64-linux', icon: 'NixOS' },
              { id: 'server-ubuntu', name: 'Ubuntu', detail: '24.04 · .deb', icon: 'Linux' },
            ]}
          />

          <div id="server-nixos-panel" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="server-nixos-tab" tabindex="0" class="mt-7" hidden>
            <p class="text-sm font-light text-nord-400">On the machine that runs the appliance.</p>

            <ol class="mt-5 space-y-5">
              <li>
                <p class={stepLabel}>1 &middot; Add the flake &mdash; <span class="normal-case tracking-normal font-mono">flake.nix</span></p>
                <pre class={code}><code>{flakeInput}</code></pre>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p class={stepLabel}>2 &middot; Import the module and turn it on &mdash; <span class="normal-case tracking-normal font-mono">configuration.nix</span></p>
                <pre class={code}><code>{serverConfig}</code></pre>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p class={stepLabel}>3 &middot; Rebuild</p>
                <pre class={code}><code>sudo nixos-rebuild switch --flake .#your-host</code></pre>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p class={stepLabel}>4 &middot; Make the first administrator &mdash; <span class="normal-case tracking-normal text-nord-600">once, on the server</span></p>
                <pre class={code}><code>neuman-admin bootstrap --name you</code></pre>

                <ul class={`mt-3 border-l border-nord-800 pl-4 ${noteList}`}>
                  <li class="flex gap-3">
                    <span class={noteDot}>&middot;</span>
                    <span>The rebuild put you in the <code class={ic}>neuman</code> group, and group membership is fixed at login: log out and back in, or the command answers <code class={ic}>Permission denied</code>.</span>
                  </li>
                  <li class="flex gap-3">
                    <span class={noteDot}>&middot;</span>
                    <span>Being root, or in <code class={ic}>wheel</code>, makes you no administrator here. Authority is a group in Neuman's own account store, which this command creates.</span>
                  </li>
                  <li class="flex gap-3">
                    <span class={noteDot}>&middot;</span>
                    <span>Every account after it is made in the administration page, with no rebuild.</span>
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </li>
            </ol>

            <ul class={`mt-6 ${noteList}`}>
              <li class="flex gap-3">
                <span class={noteDot}>&middot;</span>
                <span>Postgres, the units, the directories, the port and TLS are the module's business — what a restart changes. Accounts, roles, providers, models and the prompt are not.</span>
              </li>
              <li class="flex gap-3">
                <span class={noteDot}>&middot;</span>
                <span>An NVIDIA GPU is assumed, and CUDA is unfree, so allow it: <code class={ic}>nixpkgs.config.allowUnfree = true;</code> No GPU? Set <code class={ic}>services.neuman.gpu = "none";</code></span>
              </li>
              <li class="flex gap-3">
                <span class={noteDot}>&middot;</span>
                <span>The first build asks once whether to use <code class={ic}>cache.nixos-cuda.org</code>. Accept it, or the CUDA runtime is compiled from source.</span>
              </li>
              <li class="flex gap-3">
                <span class={noteDot}>&middot;</span>
                <span>It listens on loopback only. A client on another machine needs <code class={ic}>bind</code>, a TLS certificate and key, and the port opened.</span>
              </li>
              <li class="flex gap-3">
                <span class={noteDot}>&middot;</span>
                <span>A fresh appliance is empty — no accounts, roles, providers, models or prompt. All of it is made in the administration page, and a change needs no rebuild.</span>
              </li>
            </ul>

          </div>

          <div id="server-ubuntu-panel" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="server-ubuntu-tab" tabindex="0" class="mt-7" hidden>
            <p class="text-sm font-light text-nord-400">On Ubuntu 24.04, the server's supported baseline.</p>

            <ol class="mt-5 space-y-5">
              <li>
                <p class={stepLabel}>1 &middot; Download the package</p>
                <pre class={code}><code>{`wget https://privateai.solutions${debUrl}`}</code></pre>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p class={stepLabel}>2 &middot; Install it</p>
                <pre class={code}><code>{`sudo apt install ./${debFile}`}</code></pre>

                <ul class={`mt-3 border-l border-nord-800 pl-4 ${noteList}`}>
                  <li class="flex gap-3">
                    <span class={noteDot}>&middot;</span>
                    <span>The package creates the <code class={ic}>neuman</code> user and group, the directories, the Postgres role and database, and the systemd unit with the same sandbox the NixOS module installs.</span>
                  </li>
                  <li class="flex gap-3">
                    <span class={noteDot}>&middot;</span>
                    <span>If Postgres was not running at install time, <code class={ic}>sudo dpkg-reconfigure neuman</code> repeats the database provisioning.</span>
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p class={stepLabel}>3 &middot; Make the first administrator &mdash; <span class="normal-case tracking-normal text-nord-600">once, on the server</span></p>
                <pre class={code}><code>{`sudo usermod -aG neuman "$USER"
neuman-admin bootstrap --name you`}</code></pre>

                <ul class={`mt-3 border-l border-nord-800 pl-4 ${noteList}`}>
                  <li class="flex gap-3">
                    <span class={noteDot}>&middot;</span>
                    <span>Group membership is fixed at login: log out and back in between the two commands, or the second answers <code class={ic}>Permission denied</code>.</span>
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </li>
            </ol>

            <ul class={`mt-6 ${noteList}`}>
              <li class="flex gap-3">
                <span class={noteDot}>&middot;</span>
                <span>24.04 only, for now: the packages are built for its Python 3.12. 22.04 ships 3.10, and every release since 25.04 ships 3.13 or newer, whose interpreter cannot load the bundled wheels — <code class={ic}>apt</code> refuses rather than installing something that will not start.</span>
              </li>
              <li class="flex gap-3">
                <span class={noteDot}>&middot;</span>
                <span>The administration page is served by a client, so until one connects, administer the server with <code class={ic}>neuman-admin</code> over its socket.</span>
              </li>
              <li class="flex gap-3">
                <span class={noteDot}>&middot;</span>
                <span>Bind, port, TLS and paths are in <code class={ic}>/etc/neuman/server.env</code>. Accounts, roles, providers, models and the prompt are made in the administration page, with no restart.</span>
              </li>
              <li class="flex gap-3">
                <span class={noteDot}>&middot;</span>
                <span>Local models are not in the package: they need a <code class={ic}>llama-server</code> from somewhere else.</span>
              </li>
            </ul>

          </div>
        </div>

        <p class="mt-6 text-xs leading-relaxed text-nord-500">
          Linux only, by design. A macOS or Windows host that needs a server runs the Linux service in a container.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- The client -->
      <section class="p-6 sm:p-8 bg-nord-900 border border-nord-800 rounded-xl">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-nord-100 flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="text-frost-400 text-sm">◆</span>
          The client
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-nord-400">
          What a person works in. One per machine.
        </p>

        <div class="mt-7">
          <InstallPicker
            label="Client platform"
            installs={[
              { id: 'client-nix', name: 'Nix', detail: 'home-manager', icon: 'NixOS' },
              { id: 'client-ubuntu', name: 'Ubuntu', detail: '24.04 · .deb', icon: 'Linux' },
              { id: 'client-macos', name: 'macOS', detail: 'Coming soon', icon: 'macOS', soon: true },
            ]}
          />

          <div id="client-nix-panel" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="client-nix-tab" tabindex="0" class="mt-7" hidden>
            <p class="text-sm font-light text-nord-400">On each machine someone works at.</p>

            <ol class="mt-5 space-y-5">
              <li>
                <p class={stepLabel}>1 &middot; Add the flake, if it is not there yet</p>
                <pre class={code}><code>{flakeInput}</code></pre>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p class={stepLabel}>2 &middot; Import the module and point it at the server &mdash; <span class="normal-case tracking-normal font-mono">home.nix</span></p>
                <pre class={code}><code>{clientConfig}</code></pre>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p class={stepLabel}>3 &middot; Switch, then log in</p>
                <pre class={code}><code>home-manager switch
neuman-client login --server https://neuman.example.com --user you</code></pre>
              </li>
            </ol>

            <ul class={`mt-6 ${noteList}`}>
              <li class="flex gap-3">
                <span class={noteDot}>&middot;</span>
                <span><code class={ic}>server</code> must be <code class={ic}>https://</code> unless the appliance is this machine. The password and then the session cookie cross on every request, so the client refuses plain HTTP to another host.</span>
              </li>
              <li class="flex gap-3">
                <span class={noteDot}>&middot;</span>
                <span>It serves the chat window on <code class={ic}>http://127.0.0.1:8642</code>, and starts Chromium the first time a tool needs a browser.</span>
              </li>
              <li class="flex gap-3">
                <span class={noteDot}>&middot;</span>
                <span>The tools run as the account you are logged in as, which need not be the name of your Neuman account.</span>
              </li>
            </ul>

          </div>

          <div id="client-ubuntu-panel" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="client-ubuntu-tab" tabindex="0" class="mt-7" hidden>
            <p class="text-sm font-light text-nord-400">On a person's Ubuntu 24.04 machine, as that person.</p>

            <ol class="mt-5 space-y-5">
              <li>
                <p class={stepLabel}>1 &middot; Download the package</p>
                <pre class={code}><code>{`wget https://privateai.solutions${clientDebUrl}`}</code></pre>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p class={stepLabel}>2 &middot; Install it</p>
                <pre class={code}><code>{`sudo apt install ./${clientDebFile}`}</code></pre>
                <ul class={`mt-3 border-l border-nord-800 pl-4 ${noteList}`}>
                  <li class="flex gap-3">
                    <span class={noteDot}>&middot;</span>
                    <span>The package is built for 24.04's Python 3.12, and depends on git. Chromium is recommended, not bundled: <code class={ic}>chromium</code>, <code class={ic}>chromium-browser</code>, <code class={ic}>google-chrome</code> or the Chromium snap all work.</span>
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p class={stepLabel}>3 &middot; Log in to the appliance</p>
                <pre class={code}><code>{`neuman-client login --server https://neuman.example.com --user you`}</code></pre>

                <ul class={`mt-3 border-l border-nord-800 pl-4 ${noteList}`}>
                  <li class="flex gap-3">
                    <span class={noteDot}>&middot;</span>
                    <span>This stores the server with the session, so the services need no configuration file of their own.</span>
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p class={stepLabel}>4 &middot; Start it</p>
                <pre class={code}><code>{`systemctl --user daemon-reload
systemctl --user enable --now neuman-client.service
systemctl --user enable --now neuman-browser.service`}</code></pre>

                <ul class={`mt-3 border-l border-nord-800 pl-4 ${noteList}`}>
                  <li class="flex gap-3">
                    <span class={noteDot}>&middot;</span>
                    <span>The browser service opens the window; the Neuman entry in the application menu starts it again later.</span>
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </li>
            </ol>

            <ul class={`mt-6 ${noteList}`}>
              <li class="flex gap-3">
                <span class={noteDot}>&middot;</span>
                <span>Only the <code class={ic}>browse</code> tool needs a browser; the window itself is served on <code class={ic}>127.0.0.1:8642</code>, and the appliance is not involved in the browsing at all.</span>
              </li>
              <li class="flex gap-3">
                <span class={noteDot}>&middot;</span>
                <span><code class={ic}>neuman-client doctor</code> reports the interpreter, session, browser and socket — the first thing to run when something is wrong.</span>
              </li>
            </ul>

          </div>

          <div id="client-macos-panel" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="client-macos-tab" tabindex="0" class="mt-7" hidden>
            <div class="rounded-lg border border-dashed border-nord-700 bg-nord-950/50 p-6">
              <p class="text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-frost-400">Coming soon</p>
              <h3 class="mt-2 text-base font-medium text-nord-100">There is no macOS client to install yet.</h3>
              <p class="mt-2 text-sm font-light leading-relaxed text-nord-400">
                When it ships it will be a signed and notarised <code class={ic}>.pkg</code> with a user
                LaunchAgent, for Apple silicon on macOS 14 or later &mdash; no Nix on the Mac.
              </p>
              <p class="mt-3 text-sm font-light leading-relaxed text-nord-400">
                Until then the client runs on Linux. What differs between the two hosts is on
                <a href="/docs/platforms" class="text-frost-400 hover:text-frost-300 transition-colors">platforms</a>.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p class="mt-6 text-xs leading-relaxed text-nord-500">
          Windows is not supported, as a client or as a server.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Try before installing -->
      <section class="p-6 sm:p-8 bg-nord-950 border border-nord-800 rounded-xl">
        <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-nord-100 flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="text-frost-400 text-sm">◆</span>
          Or see it first
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm font-light text-nord-400 leading-relaxed">
          A live image that boots straight into a running appliance, with nothing to install:
        </p>
        <pre class={code}><code>nix build github:lukebodmer/neuman#iso</code></pre>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm font-light text-nord-400 leading-relaxed">
          The ISO lands in <code class={ic}>result/iso/</code>. It is a demonstration, not a
          deployment: everything runs in RAM, there is no TLS, and the password is public.
        </p>
      </section>

    </div>

    <p class="mt-10 text-sm font-light text-nord-400 leading-relaxed">
      The long version, in the order things go wrong: the
      <a href="/docs/setup" class="text-frost-400 hover:text-frost-300 transition-colors">setup guide</a>.
      What runs where: <a href="/docs/platforms" class="text-frost-400 hover:text-frost-300 transition-colors">platforms</a>.
    </p>

  </main>
</Base>
